ActivityLog.pm:229 reads the ENTIRE log ref and writes it back with one line appended, on every mutating command. Every CAS retry re-reads the whole blob as well.

Measured on a fresh board: 93 bytes per log entry. The sum of all log blobs ever written after N commands is therefore ~93*N^2/2 raw — at N=10,000 that is about 4.6 GB of blobs (before zlib) for a log that is only ~1 MB.

Compounding it: _commit_for_content (Git.pm:706) writes PARENTLESS commits, so every ref update turns its predecessor into garbage immediately. Measured after 105 commands: 726 loose objects, 666 of them unreachable (92%), i.e. ~6.3 unreachable objects per mutating command.

Consequence measured: past ~6,700 loose objects (roughly 1,060 mutating commands) git gc --auto fires on every commit/fetch/push but cannot remove the unreachable objects (gc.pruneExpire 2 weeks) and writes no gc.log to suppress further runs. Verified: the state persists indefinitely. Each run is cheap (ms) — this is a disk/slowdown problem, not a CPU one.

Options worth weighing: append-only per-entry refs or a segmented/rotating log, so a write is O(1) instead of O(n).
2026-08-15 09:04 Chose the segmented log (option b), not one ref per entry (a). Per-entry refs make the write O(1) but the ref namespace unbounded: every list_refs walk (karr log, karr context, sync's ref_oids, push/fetch enumeration) becomes O(entries), reading the log turns 1 blob read into 3 object reads per entry, and the loose-object cost per entry is unchanged. Segments bound the write by a cap instead of by the history while keeping the ref count at ~1 per 80 entries.

Layout: refs/karr/log/<role>/<email> is segment 0, refs/karr/log/<role>/<email>+000001 the next. '+' is legal in a ref name and _encode_component never emits one (it becomes %2B), so no collision with another identity; segments are siblings of segment 0, not children, because git cannot hold a ref and a directory of the same name -- .../<email>/000001 would be unwritable on every board that already has a log. Only the newest segment is appended to, up to segment_max_bytes (8 KiB), guarded by write_ref_cas inside retry_contended; rotation uses expected_old => undef, so two writers rotating at once cannot clobber each other.

No marker ref, no repair pass: unlike refs/karr/meta/encoding, which recorded a fact the content could not tell you, this layout IS the ref names. A pre-#171 board has segment 0 and nothing else -- exactly what an unrotated log looks like -- so it is read and appended to unchanged; pre-#75 legacy refs still merge in ahead of it. last_entry now walks refs newest-first and stops at the first with an entry (one ref read, not the whole log). karr log / karr context see segments through the refs/karr/log/* walk they already do; context's self-exclusion moved from '$ref eq $self_ref' to ActivityLog->owns_ref, or an agent's own rotated history would have read as another agent's.

Measured (fresh board, ~100-byte entries): before, bytes of objects per entry grew 5.4K/10.5K/20.8K at 100/200/400 entries; after, flat at ~4.2K -- 1000 entries went from ~51 MB projected to 4.26 MB, and wall clock from 6.2s to 1.65s. Test t/171 measures 305 vs 297 bytes/entry at 60 vs 120 entries (quadratic would double it).

